# Complexity Analysis

## Brute Force

| Complexity | Value     |
| ---------- | --------- |
| Time       | **O(n²)** |
| Space      | **O(n)**  |

---

## Optimal

| Complexity  | Value                                                                                                                                 |
| ----------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| Sorting     | **O(n log n)**                                                                                                                        |
| Traversal   | **O(n)**                                                                                                                              |
| Total Time  | **O(n log n)**                                                                                                                        |
| Extra Space | **O(1)** (ignoring the sorting algorithm's internal stack; **O(log n)** for Python's Timsort recursion/merge bookkeeping in practice) |

---

# Key Interview Takeaways

* Unsorted arrays often become much simpler after **sorting**.
* Count **gaps** instead of explicitly constructing subsets when only the number of subsets is required.
* Since all elements are **distinct**, every break between adjacent sorted numbers directly starts a new subset.
* The dominant cost is sorting, giving an overall time complexity of **O(n log n)**.
